From e10c88f60e878c0e78887d47c1a7ceabde9ac051 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Micheal Waltz <micheal@ecliptik.com>
Date: Fri, 25 Sep 2026 11:45:50 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] DOS-PORT: patch 0218 -- AUDIOPRB times the audio poll only
 when SDL_HINT_DOS_AUDIO_DIAG_TIMING is set

Why: ags 0059's temporary AUDIOPRB diagnostic (with 0061/0062/0069-0073) reads uclock() three times per
audio_core_entry_poll() call, about 6 unguarded PIT reads a tick on the 486 in every build (LAB-MMD 44,
~26 us/tick), plus the miditick and prepsong brackets. No current gate or scorer reads it: lab-wb and
lab-journeys use none of it, and lab-metric's three readers (lab-d2, lab-d2hw, lab-s32f1) are closed
D2-era scorers whose cells are already scored. The per-call timing is the cost, not the file, so an
end-of-run dump alone would not help.

What: the poll, the miditick bracket and the prepsong bracket read the clock and snapshot SDL's
diagnostics only when SDL 0199's switch is on (SDL_DOSAudioDiagTiming(), default off). The engine
latches it in engine_init_audio(), on the main thread before any audio exists, because the OPL write it
also gates can run from the IRQ-0 pump. With it off, the poll is only counted, and RUNMANI.LOG gets one
"DOS-PORT audioprb timing=off total_calls=N" line instead of zero-cost windows. With it on, the windows
are unchanged (the D2-era scorers still parse them) plus one "pump_hist" line: whole-run poll count,
pump and slot totals, and a per-call pump histogram (< 1, 1-5, 5-9, >= 9 ms), which never drops
anything, unlike the 64-window cap (lab-metric's suggestion).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
